<h2>A case that climbed the entire ladder</h2>
<p>Two widows, Suman and Shakuntla, were found dead on the night of 17 April 2011. FIR No. 23 of 2011 at P.S. Baund Kalan named the appellant under Section 302 IPC on the basis of a statement by a witness who had not seen the incident. The trial court convicted both accused in 2013 and sentenced them to death; the Punjab and Haryana High Court, in the murder reference, confirmed the appellant's conviction under Sections 302, 449, 297 and 201 IPC but commuted the death sentence to life, while acquitting the co-accused. The appeals reached the Supreme Court through the Supreme Court Legal Services Committee, leave being granted in 2014; the Bench of Justice Satish Chandra Sharma and Justice Nongmeikapam Kotiswar Singh decided them on 16 November 2026.</p>

<h2>What the prosecution was left with</h2>
<p>At trial, the private witnesses turned hostile. The case then rested on their Section 161 statements, disclosure statements, the recovery of a danda and a gandasi, and forensic reports that were inconclusive.</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>The conviction's logic</h3><p>The witnesses had told the police a coherent story; their police statements, the recoveries and the post-mortem could together carry the case despite the courtroom retractions.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>The Supreme Court's holding</h3><p>Section 162 CrPC bars use of police statements "for any purpose" except contradiction; a proved contradiction impeaches credit under Section 155(3) of the Evidence Act but proves nothing. With the recoveries not proved under Section 27 and circumstances not put under Section 313, no legal evidence remained.</p></div>
</div>

<p>The Court traced the rule to <em>Tahsildar Singh v. State of U.P.</em> and <em>V.K. Mishra v. State of Uttarakhand</em> (paras 31-33), noted that statements under Section 164 CrPC stand on a better footing than Section 161 statements (para 34), and dismantled the prosecution's reliance on a key witness's statement recorded five days after the incident (paras 37-38). The concurrent findings were interfered with; the convictions and sentences under Sections 302, 449, 297 and 201 were set aside and the appellant acquitted, with the trial court's compensation direction also vacated — and the DLSA, Bhiwani asked to consider the victims' family under the compensation scheme now housed in Section 396 BNSS.</p>

<h2>The doctrinal core</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Material</th><th>Permissible use at trial</th></tr>
<tr><td>Section 161 CrPC / Section 180 BNSS statement</td><td>Contradiction of the maker only — never substantive proof</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proved contradiction</td><td>Impeaches credit (Evidence Act s.155(3) / BSA equivalent); does not prove the earlier version</td></tr>
<tr><td>Section 164 CrPC / Section 183 BNSS statement</td><td>Better footing, but still not substantive evidence by itself</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disclosure and recovery</td><td>Admissible only to the extent proved under Evidence Act s.27 discipline</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For appellate practice:</strong> where a conviction survives on hostile-witness records, the appeal should isolate each item the courts below treated as evidence and ask the statutory question — under what provision is this admissible, and for what purpose? Convictions that cannot answer that question for their load-bearing material are vulnerable even at the Article 136 stage, concurrent findings notwithstanding.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The judgment is also a sobering institutional document: a man sentenced to death in 2013 has been acquitted thirteen years after the trial began, because the legal character of police statements was mistaken at every stage below. The discipline of Sections 161 and 162 — carried into the BNSS — exists precisely to prevent investigation records from quietly becoming proof.</p>
</div>
